  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $S=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{aligned}]$, then ${S}^{3}=$",
  "worked_solution": "Computing ${S}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{2} & =S\\cdot S \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}10 & 5 \\\\ -20 & -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, since ${S}^{2}=O,$ the matrix $S$ is a nilpotent matrix of index $2.$ \n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${S}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{3} & ={S}^{2}\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\cdot S \\\\  & =O\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
